WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.544
- Mo Williams literally left.

00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:03.920
Nobody knew where he went.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.090
And then came back and played
in Game 5 of the NBA Finals—
- Yeah.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.842
- And was important to us.

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.885
Dahntay Jones played
in the Finals.

00:00:10.885 --> 00:00:14.014
Like, I remember vividly Coach
going, "Dahntay, I need you."

00:00:14.014 --> 00:00:15.432
And he rips his stuff off.

00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:16.641
Hasn't played a minute.

00:00:16.641 --> 00:00:19.436
- Dahntay Jones was
big for Game 6.

00:00:19.436 --> 00:00:19.936
- Intregal.

00:00:19.936 --> 00:00:20.603
Is that the word?
- He was—

00:00:20.603 --> 00:00:21.938
- Intregal.

00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:22.439
- Intregal.

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:23.231
- Integral. There we go.

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:24.774
That's a good word.

00:00:24.774 --> 00:00:26.735
In-tre-gal.

00:00:26.735 --> 00:00:29.696
♪♪♪

00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:39.289
 What up, guys?

00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:42.375
Today, welcome to "Full-Bodied"
with my sorta good friend,

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:43.251
Kevin Love.

00:00:43.251 --> 00:00:44.961
As the wines get
darker, the questions

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:45.962
are gonna get deeper.

00:00:45.962 --> 00:00:48.381
Let's first open this Moet up.

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:50.717
I got this Moet, honestly, because I'm

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:51.760
pretty proud of you, man.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:52.969
This is, what, Year 14?

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:54.345
- I'll be heading
into 14 next year.

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:55.972
- You know, we gotta
celebrate each year.

00:00:55.972 --> 00:00:56.473
- I love it.

00:00:56.473 --> 00:00:59.976
- This year has been
wild, to say the least.

00:00:59.976 --> 00:01:01.102
Let's start like this.

00:01:01.102 --> 00:01:03.438
Like, growing up in Lake
Oswego, Oregon,

00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:07.650
like, when did you discover that
basketball was your thing?

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:08.902
Didn't you play baseball too?

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:10.487
- Played baseball,
played Little League.

00:01:10.487 --> 00:01:12.322
I was a pitcher.

00:01:12.322 --> 00:01:15.283
But, you know, my true love
was with basketball.

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:16.910
My dad played in the NBA.
- Right.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:17.869
- Looked up to my dad

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:18.620
as my hero.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.955
He had me on the phone
when I was young with, you know, guys

00:01:20.955 --> 00:01:22.165
like Connie Hawkins.

00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:24.084
He showed me Jerry West,
who he played with.

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:25.627
Gail Goodrich. Happy Hairston.

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:27.754
Elmore Smith, blocked
17 shots in a game.

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:30.715
Elvin Hayes. Earl the
Pearl. Wes Unseld,

00:01:30.715 --> 00:01:33.176
who I'm named after—
my middle name, Wesley.

00:01:33.176 --> 00:01:34.636
Some of the greatest
of all time.

00:01:34.636 --> 00:01:35.804
So basketball was always it.

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:37.639
- Being there for
14 years—there's

00:01:37.639 --> 00:01:39.224
a lotta guys,
actually, from Portland—
- Yeah.

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:41.392
- Who are just hoopers,
like Terrence Ross.

00:01:41.392 --> 00:01:42.143
That was like—

00:01:42.143 --> 00:01:42.769
I saw him play.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:45.563
I was like, "This kid has no
business being in high school.

00:01:45.563 --> 00:01:46.731
Like, he has no business—

00:01:46.731 --> 00:01:47.565
like, he's a pro.

00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:48.858
You can see it right there."

00:01:48.858 --> 00:01:53.154
So your transition from
high school to UCLA—

00:01:53.154 --> 00:01:54.489
like, why UCLA?

00:01:54.489 --> 00:01:57.826
What was it about that
stupid school that you liked?

00:01:57.826 --> 00:02:01.121
And also, what was it like
playing with Russell Westbrook

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:02.163
in your stacked team?

00:02:02.163 --> 00:02:06.126
And I think you guys may
or may not have won once,

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:06.835
but I don't know.

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:07.752
I didn't do the research.

00:02:07.752 --> 00:02:08.169
But go ahead.

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:09.420
- Did you guys win a
national championship

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.630
when you were there?

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.007
- Uh, yeah, we did, because we had—
it's a better school.

00:02:13.007 --> 00:02:14.759
So we had to let
somebody else win.

00:02:14.759 --> 00:02:15.301
- Right, right. No, understood.

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:17.345
I ended up picking UCLA
because, going back

00:02:17.345 --> 00:02:19.764
to my dad for a second,
Morningside High School.

00:02:19.764 --> 00:02:22.642
My dad's assistant high
school coach was Jim Harrick.

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:25.103
So we started going to
the games. All my family

00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:26.187
from Southern California,

00:02:26.187 --> 00:02:29.190
we would go to Pauley Pavilion—
- Yeah, yeah. Old-school Pauley.
- And watch Jim Harrick

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.108
and those teams play.

00:02:30.108 --> 00:02:32.694
It was amazing to
see because, you know, there

00:02:32.694 --> 00:02:34.821
was great pros
throughout the Pac-10.

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:37.699
And I always thought it'd be
great to play close to home—

00:02:37.699 --> 00:02:39.033
- Right, right.
- Or close to family.

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:40.869
And my top two schools
at the end of the day

00:02:40.869 --> 00:02:42.412
were North Carolina,
which is crazy.

00:02:42.412 --> 00:02:45.415
Roy Williams—I mean,
finally, yeah, he hung it up.

00:02:45.415 --> 00:02:45.915
- Yeah.

00:02:45.915 --> 00:02:46.708
- It's crazy he—

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:47.542
after all that time.

00:02:47.542 --> 00:02:48.918
So those were my
top two schools.

00:02:48.918 --> 00:02:50.211
For me, it made the most sense.

00:02:50.211 --> 00:02:52.505
Two back-to-back Final Fours,
and we ended up making it

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:54.215
to a third straight Final Four.

00:02:54.215 --> 00:02:56.426
Lost to Memphis, which,
all four No. 1 seeds.

00:02:56.426 --> 00:02:59.137
Kansas and North
Carolina were in there.

00:02:59.137 --> 00:03:01.806
And that was an incredible year
but didn't get the job done.

00:03:01.806 --> 00:03:04.100
Russ and I left, and we were
the fourth and fifth pick.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.394
- Did you see the evolution
of Russell Westbrook

00:03:06.394 --> 00:03:10.607
from then, who was just
this, like, downhill runner, just

00:03:10.607 --> 00:03:15.862
attacking the rack, to this guy
that's now completely, like, crushing

00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:16.404
the NBA?

00:03:16.404 --> 00:03:17.530
- So it was crazy.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.073
Russ and I, we had
known each other.

00:03:19.073 --> 00:03:20.658
And I know he had come
from Leuzinger High School.

00:03:20.658 --> 00:03:22.410
He didn't dunk till he was
a junior in high school.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:22.911
- What?

00:03:22.911 --> 00:03:23.661
- Which is crazy.

00:03:23.661 --> 00:03:27.415
His senior year, Jordan Farmar
ended up going to the league,

00:03:27.415 --> 00:03:28.833
so opened up a spot at UCLA.
- Yeah.

00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:30.960
- I think his other school
might have been Creighton.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:31.544
- Oh, wow.

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:33.296
- Russ ends up at UCLA.
Didn't play much.

00:03:33.296 --> 00:03:35.673
I think maybe 11 minutes
his freshman year.

00:03:35.673 --> 00:03:36.466
I remember watching him.

00:03:36.466 --> 00:03:38.927
I think they played Kansas,
and he had a really great game.

00:03:38.927 --> 00:03:41.763
But really just defensive
player, and then just

00:03:41.763 --> 00:03:43.181
make some plays
happen on offense.

00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:45.892
But he wasn't a primary focus
on either side of the ball.

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:48.019
So fast forward, the
summer when I got there.

00:03:48.019 --> 00:03:49.812
The first time I saw
him in the men's gym—

00:03:49.812 --> 00:03:52.315
and this was at the time when, like,
KG was in there, Paul Pierce.

00:03:52.315 --> 00:03:53.816
Kobe would come in.

00:03:53.816 --> 00:03:55.777
And I can remember
Russ just kinda taking it

00:03:55.777 --> 00:03:59.030
off the top of the glass, you know,
coming down, surveying

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:02.242
the court, you know, kinda cross-grained
into the lane.

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:05.036
You know, kinda spun back and went
up with his left hand and was,

00:04:05.036 --> 00:04:06.746
like, top of the
square and finished.

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:07.997
And I thought, "Wow.

00:04:07.997 --> 00:04:09.040
He's gonna be special."

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.502
And the best thing, I
think, that happened to him,

00:04:12.502 --> 00:04:15.588
and maybe even our team,
was that, unfortunately,

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:18.132
Darren Collison had an
MCL sprain in his knee.

00:04:18.132 --> 00:04:19.842
Russell had to play point guard.

00:04:19.842 --> 00:04:21.052
So he had to handle the ball.

00:04:21.052 --> 00:04:22.178
He had to guide the team.

00:04:22.178 --> 00:04:23.221
He had to run the team.

00:04:23.221 --> 00:04:26.224
Once he moved to that
shooting guard position

00:04:26.224 --> 00:04:28.101
and I was able to outlet
to both those guys,

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:30.603
those guys were able to, were all able
to play off each other—

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.146
Luc Mbah a Moute, Josh Shipp.

00:04:32.146 --> 00:04:34.107
I mean, we had a number
of players on that team

00:04:34.107 --> 00:04:35.275
that could really play.
- Good gracious.

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:35.984
That's a squad.

00:04:35.984 --> 00:04:36.693
- That's a squad.

00:04:36.693 --> 00:04:38.152
- We're at the Red Hook Winery.

00:04:38.152 --> 00:04:41.614
And so this is an Abe Schoener
Jamesport Vineyard Sauvignon

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:42.115
Blanc.

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:43.491
They make a really good SB.

00:04:43.491 --> 00:04:44.492
It has melon notes.

00:04:44.492 --> 00:04:45.285
It's refreshing.

00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:47.537
So we talked about
Russ' transformation.

00:04:47.537 --> 00:04:49.622
We wanna talk about
your transformation, right?

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:55.545
You went from a big boy—
- Yeah, OK, OK. I thought you—
- With a full chinstrap

00:04:55.545 --> 00:04:59.048
- Yep.
- To all of a sudden, you're
on, like, the ESPN "The Body Issue."

00:04:59.048 --> 00:05:00.300
How was that evolution?

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.386
And then, like, you
were a walking bucket.

00:05:03.386 --> 00:05:06.597
You were getting 20-and-20
as the old you,

00:05:06.597 --> 00:05:08.725
but then your efficiency
was still there.

00:05:08.725 --> 00:05:10.226
And a lotta guys can't do that.

00:05:10.226 --> 00:05:10.435
- Yeah.

00:05:10.435 --> 00:05:11.561
Well, first, I'll say this.

00:05:11.561 --> 00:05:14.439
Like, I've had my fair share
of injuries in my career,

00:05:14.439 --> 00:05:17.900
but I was looking at being
270 pounds and thinking that

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:21.070
wasn't sustainable—
- Right, right, right.
- At 6'8.5", like—

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:23.406
it just wasn't sustainable
for me to be that heavy.

00:05:23.406 --> 00:05:25.241
I'm thinking, "I
can't play 82 games.

00:05:25.241 --> 00:05:26.993
I can't play four
games in five nights.

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:29.871
I can't go play Dallas—"
- No.
- "Fly back and play

00:05:29.871 --> 00:05:31.789
back-to-back being that heavy."

00:05:31.789 --> 00:05:34.876
And I think I got away with it
because I was in my early 20s.

00:05:34.876 --> 00:05:37.128
But I knew that long-term,
longevity-wise,

00:05:37.128 --> 00:05:39.547
and being able to stay in
the league, especially with—

00:05:39.547 --> 00:05:40.965
and my dad had the
presence of mind.

00:05:40.965 --> 00:05:43.801
He's like, "You gotta
be able to shoot the ball.

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:44.886
- Yeah.
- "I know you have the touch.

00:05:44.886 --> 00:05:45.970
I know you have the capability.

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:47.221
I know you have the skill set."

00:05:47.221 --> 00:05:48.806
It was the same thing with
you being a stretch 5.

00:05:48.806 --> 00:05:49.265
- Right, right.

00:05:49.265 --> 00:05:52.226
- If you don't have that today,
you might not see the floor.

00:05:52.226 --> 00:05:54.771
There's very few big men.

00:05:54.771 --> 00:05:57.106
The post-ups are 50 percent down
in the last six years.

00:05:57.106 --> 00:05:57.607
- Yeah.

00:05:57.607 --> 00:05:59.192
I just think the
evolution of the game

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:03.112
is, like, there are more big
men that can shoot and stretch

00:06:03.112 --> 00:06:05.031
the floor
- Yes.
- than there
are true point guards.

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:06.699
- No, and if you're able
to stretch the floor,

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:08.785
it's just such an
asset for a team,

00:06:08.785 --> 00:06:12.205
especially knowing how hard
it is to guard those guys.
- Yeah.

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:13.081
- It's impossible.

00:06:13.081 --> 00:06:15.416
But I think what my
father understood

00:06:15.416 --> 00:06:18.002
and what I understood was
that the game was changing.
- Yeah, yeah. Oh, big time.

00:06:18.002 --> 00:06:21.339
- And it was becoming a
pace-and-space type league.

00:06:21.339 --> 00:06:22.715
- When I retired
from basketball, I

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:25.426
realized that wine was my
dream outside of the dream.

00:06:25.426 --> 00:06:27.053
And I also realized
I had to do it

00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:30.056
my way by creating my own wine
label, Chosen Family Wines.

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:31.724
And in this segment,
we're gonna dive

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:35.061
into my guest's dreams,
brought to you by Porsche.

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:38.356
So was there more pressure
being a lineage kid

00:06:38.356 --> 00:06:40.608
than you think the
other kids—like, being, you know,

00:06:40.608 --> 00:06:42.318
no offense, the white guy?

00:06:42.318 --> 00:06:44.737
You're athletic, but you're
not, like, you don't jump out at

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:46.072
the gym.

00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:49.283
Like, most of your highlight videos
were you throwing outlet passes.
- Right.

00:06:49.283 --> 00:06:52.370
- And getting rebounds.
- [Laughing]

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:55.957
- Like, how does that, like, growing up that
way, how did that motivate you?

00:06:55.957 --> 00:06:59.377
- I always talk about, like, we play
in the best league in the world

00:06:59.377 --> 00:07:00.628
cuz it's so diverse.

00:07:00.628 --> 00:07:04.173
Even dating back to my
childhood, my dad taking me,

00:07:04.173 --> 00:07:06.592
having the presence of mind
and understanding cuz it

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:07.427
was the same way with him.

00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:09.095
He grew up in
Inglewood, California,

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:11.889
went to Morningside
High School and played

00:07:11.889 --> 00:07:14.851
against, you know, a lot of teams that
were predominantly Black.
- Right, right.

00:07:14.851 --> 00:07:17.520
- And he knew that if he
was going to get better,

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:18.855
he had to play against the best.
- Right.

00:07:18.855 --> 00:07:23.401
- And, you know, the league has been built on
the shoulders of Black players.
- Right.

00:07:23.401 --> 00:07:25.361
- So he knew that taking myself—

00:07:25.361 --> 00:07:27.613
and at the time, it was
Northeast Portland—injecting

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:30.199
me into, you know, Jefferson High
School, or Grant High

00:07:30.199 --> 00:07:32.660
School, Lincoln High
School.
- Yeah, yeah.
- PIL schools

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:34.245
were in the inner
city. He said,

00:07:34.245 --> 00:07:37.123
you have to play there
in order to get better.

00:07:37.123 --> 00:07:39.292
It ended up being myself
and my best friend,
- Right.

00:07:39.292 --> 00:07:40.877
Ernie Spada. Eventually
Kyle Singler,

00:07:40.877 --> 00:07:42.462
who played in the league
for a number of years,

00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:43.379
- Yeah, Kyle, comeback.

00:07:43.379 --> 00:07:46.674
- Who, you know, were the only
white guys on the team.
- Yeah.

00:07:46.674 --> 00:07:50.636
- And from that point
on, it just became, you know, kinda part

00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:52.763
of who I am, what I'm about, is that,

00:07:52.763 --> 00:07:54.474
you know, I knew if I was
gonna be the best,

00:07:54.474 --> 00:07:55.558
I had to play against the best.

00:07:55.558 --> 00:07:56.934
And that happened
to be the case.

00:07:56.934 --> 00:07:58.936
You know, we're in such a
hypermasculine sport.

00:07:58.936 --> 00:08:01.481
And it's both men
and women's
- Totally.
- Like, sports.

00:08:01.481 --> 00:08:02.398
I really believe that.

00:08:02.398 --> 00:08:05.067
Or it could be, you know, you
could work in finance.

00:08:05.067 --> 00:08:06.944
You could work at a
Fortune 500 company.

00:08:06.944 --> 00:08:09.322
Like, no matter what walk
of life you're in,

00:08:09.322 --> 00:08:11.866
withholding compassion
does nobody any good.

00:08:11.866 --> 00:08:13.993
Like, you're more empathetic,
you're more evolved,

00:08:13.993 --> 00:08:16.245
you're a better listener, like,
it creates community.

00:08:16.245 --> 00:08:17.288
It creates understanding.

00:08:17.288 --> 00:08:19.707
So, I mean, that's what I've learned.

00:08:19.707 --> 00:08:22.960
I'm, again, on the court, outside
of it, I'm not perfect at all.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:27.590
I just hope, like, if I could
just help one kid, you know, early

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:29.550
intervention, get in
there and say, "Listen,

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.928
the brain is changing so
much" before behavior.

00:08:31.928 --> 00:08:35.723
Like, if I can help that
one kid, listen, I'm good.

00:08:35.723 --> 00:08:38.392
If I help myself,
I help somebody,

00:08:38.392 --> 00:08:41.479
I'm living life out in the open,
I'm an open book, I'm good.

00:08:41.479 --> 00:08:42.772
- Well, I think you've done that.

00:08:42.772 --> 00:08:45.316
I think, what I thought
was, OK, well, I'm gonna

00:08:45.316 --> 00:08:47.568
just deal with
this for this season,

00:08:47.568 --> 00:08:49.654
and it's been ongoing, right?

00:08:49.654 --> 00:08:52.657
There are times where I'm Mr.
Happy-Go-Lucky, to be honest.

00:08:52.657 --> 00:08:53.866
I'm a glass-half-full dude.

00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:57.453
But I feel like, you know, seeing
you guys, whether it's you

00:08:57.453 --> 00:08:59.539
or DeMar DeRozan
or Dak Prescott,

00:08:59.539 --> 00:09:04.126
I think more kids need
to be lifted up mentally.

00:09:04.126 --> 00:09:06.796
A sport is not end-all, be-all.

00:09:06.796 --> 00:09:08.673
Like, we, sometimes we forget.

00:09:08.673 --> 00:09:11.300
Like, it's weird as an adult
who's played in the pros,

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.678
watching my son play and
then watch the other parents and

00:09:13.678 --> 00:09:15.888
how they talk to their kid.

00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:16.681
"You didn't," you know, "You didn't do this!"

00:09:16.681 --> 00:09:18.766
And I'm like, "Hey, Hendrix,
did you get sweaty?"

00:09:18.766 --> 00:09:20.101
I'm like, awesome, dude.
- [Laughing]

00:09:20.101 --> 00:09:21.894
- Like, I don't care.

00:09:21.894 --> 00:09:24.689
Like, are you enjoying your
time with your peers?

00:09:24.689 --> 00:09:28.818
Are you understanding
the value of, like, camaraderie?

00:09:28.818 --> 00:09:36.200
Sports should be an outlet and
a place where people can grow

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:37.743
and not a place
where you're supposed

00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:40.413
to tuck your feelings away.

00:09:40.413 --> 00:09:42.123
But something
awesome has happened.

00:09:42.123 --> 00:09:44.041
And you see our whole lineup.

00:09:44.041 --> 00:09:48.212
When I say our, I mean, you are
now part of the Chosen Family.

00:09:48.212 --> 00:09:49.255
So let's talk about, like,

00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:50.798
when did you get into wine?

00:09:50.798 --> 00:09:53.759
Why did you want to get into
wine with me and with us

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:54.719
at Chosen?

00:09:54.719 --> 00:09:56.762
- You know, I grew up just outside
of Portland, Oregon.

00:09:56.762 --> 00:09:59.557
And obviously, if you've been
to Dundee Hills, Willamette

00:09:59.557 --> 00:10:02.101
Valley, so on and so
forth in that region,

00:10:02.101 --> 00:10:05.229
it's obviously become
more recognized as a place

00:10:05.229 --> 00:10:09.734
where wine and that culture
is continuing to grow.

00:10:09.734 --> 00:10:14.280
But when I was growing up, you know, it
was all pinot and chard grapes.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:16.115
So that's what I was
first introduced to.

00:10:16.115 --> 00:10:19.368
And it wasn't until my third
or fourth year in the league.
- Yeah.

00:10:19.368 --> 00:10:22.913
- I mean, I came in at 19 years
old, started my rookie season

00:10:22.913 --> 00:10:23.706
at 20.

00:10:23.706 --> 00:10:26.167
But, yeah, my wine journey
continued really

00:10:26.167 --> 00:10:29.754
through my aunt on
my dad's side who

00:10:29.754 --> 00:10:31.797
introduced me to Napa cabs.

00:10:31.797 --> 00:10:34.550
So she introduced me to a
lot of California grapes,

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:35.635
primarily being cabs.

00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:38.179
So I initially just started
drinking a lot of cab.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:10:38.179 --> 00:10:42.767
- And I think, really, in 2015-16,
we started drinking that.

00:10:42.767 --> 00:10:44.060
But then you came on the team.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:44.935
- Yeah.

00:10:44.935 --> 00:10:46.395
- We started bringing new bottles.

00:10:46.395 --> 00:10:47.938
We started bringing
different shiraz.

00:10:47.938 --> 00:10:50.733
We just started bringing
different pinots, chardonnays,

00:10:50.733 --> 00:10:51.776
all across the board.

00:10:51.776 --> 00:10:52.652
- Yeah, yeah, yeah. Italians, God...

00:10:52.652 --> 00:10:53.611
- We were going old world, new world.

00:10:53.611 --> 00:10:54.111
Exactly.

00:10:54.111 --> 00:10:55.237
- Oh, good gracious.

00:10:55.237 --> 00:10:58.658
- And I think that opened
me up to understanding

00:10:58.658 --> 00:11:01.952
that, OK, the more I know, the
more I realize I don't know.

00:11:01.952 --> 00:11:03.162
But boy, I love this.

00:11:03.162 --> 00:11:05.831
This is, like you said, a
connector for so many people.

00:11:05.831 --> 00:11:07.500
We would start bringing
it on the plane

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.378
and think, yo, why aren't
you giving me a glass?

00:11:10.378 --> 00:11:12.213
Why can't I have a red
cup and drink this?
- Right, right.

00:11:12.213 --> 00:11:14.298
- And we weren't snooty about, we
weren't snobby about it at all.

00:11:14.298 --> 00:11:15.508
Then we would go to dinner.

00:11:15.508 --> 00:11:16.050
- Oh, God.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.636
- We would order more bottles.
- Yeah, oh.

00:11:18.636 --> 00:11:19.804
- And you said it funny.

00:11:19.804 --> 00:11:24.100
Like, you would do something, you know, super
obscure, something different.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:25.893
You know, I would bring,
obviously, what I love,

00:11:25.893 --> 00:11:28.646
which is mostly, like, cabs and stuff
that was heavy at the time.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:11:28.646 --> 00:11:30.356
- Maybe some pinots
here and there.

00:11:30.356 --> 00:11:31.774
That created a culture with us.

00:11:31.774 --> 00:11:33.818
I think outside of
maybe Melo and a couple

00:11:33.818 --> 00:11:36.112
other players, that
culture of wine

00:11:36.112 --> 00:11:38.364
wasn't quite there
yet until, honestly, I

00:11:38.364 --> 00:11:40.241
think we took it over
the top and started it.

00:11:40.241 --> 00:11:40.825
- Yeah.

00:11:40.825 --> 00:11:41.992
This has been a
long time coming.

00:11:41.992 --> 00:11:43.536
We talked about it
before I retired.

00:11:43.536 --> 00:11:45.162
- You know, we sat next to each
other on the plane.

00:11:45.162 --> 00:11:47.498
If we wanted to start a
wine brand, first of all,

00:11:47.498 --> 00:11:48.791
obviously, do it in Portland.

00:11:48.791 --> 00:11:50.793
There's a storytelling
component there.

00:11:50.793 --> 00:11:52.294
There's a family
component there.

00:11:52.294 --> 00:11:53.212
Like, what would we name it?

00:11:53.212 --> 00:11:56.465
So we started going through and
listing out different stuff.

00:11:56.465 --> 00:11:58.634
You know, your parties at
the end of the year
- Oh, God.

00:11:58.634 --> 00:12:01.303
- were all based around wine,
- Yeah.
- brought everybody together

00:12:01.303 --> 00:12:02.304
collectively.

00:12:02.304 --> 00:12:03.639
It was super cool.

00:12:03.639 --> 00:12:07.935
And I loved what
yourself, Jake, Chase,

00:12:07.935 --> 00:12:10.438
visiting L'Angolo,
visiting that area,

00:12:10.438 --> 00:12:11.689
and starting Chosen Family.

00:12:11.689 --> 00:12:13.482
I just, you guys were
doing something different

00:12:13.482 --> 00:12:14.442
that I wanted to be a part of.

00:12:14.442 --> 00:12:16.444
And it's funny we're
sitting here drinking wine,

00:12:16.444 --> 00:12:18.237
because that, that all started

00:12:18.237 --> 00:12:18.821
- Oh.

00:12:18.821 --> 00:12:19.780
- once you got there.

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:21.240
And I'm talking about...

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:22.116
[Cork popping]
- Oh, it started.

00:12:22.116 --> 00:12:22.658
- Yeah.

00:12:22.658 --> 00:12:25.286
We didn't realize
exactly what we had.

00:12:25.286 --> 00:12:26.996
What we had was so special.

00:12:26.996 --> 00:12:28.914
Like, I look back, and
I think when you

00:12:28.914 --> 00:12:32.251
reach the pinnacle, when you're
at the top, you want that.

00:12:32.251 --> 00:12:34.545
It's like watching the
last, it's like a drug.

00:12:34.545 --> 00:12:35.129
- Yeah.

00:12:35.129 --> 00:12:36.005
- You want that.

00:12:36.005 --> 00:12:38.090
And you want that
every single year.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:41.302
So to come from a situation
like you were to come into that

00:12:41.302 --> 00:12:43.137
and win a championship,
it's life-changing.

00:12:43.137 --> 00:12:43.888
- Oh, for sure, it
was life-changing.

00:12:43.888 --> 00:12:45.222
- It's a life-changing experience.

00:12:45.222 --> 00:12:51.061
We were the most talented
underachieving regular-season

00:12:51.061 --> 00:12:52.605
team of all time.

00:12:52.605 --> 00:12:53.105
- Oh, easily.

00:12:53.105 --> 00:12:54.064
- Without question.

00:12:54.064 --> 00:12:55.649
We didn't care
until the playoffs.

00:12:55.649 --> 00:12:57.067
- Did not care.
- No.

00:12:57.067 --> 00:12:59.403
- We would lose
games and be like—
- Right.
- [Sighing] All right, what's next?

00:12:59.403 --> 00:13:02.782
- Honestly, we were
"Days of our Lives."
- [Laughing]

00:13:02.782 --> 00:13:04.658
- We had so many storylines.

00:13:04.658 --> 00:13:06.327
We thrived under chaos.

00:13:06.327 --> 00:13:08.871
But when it came
time, we had fun.

00:13:08.871 --> 00:13:10.247
And we had the best time.

00:13:10.247 --> 00:13:10.956
Like, it was the best—

00:13:10.956 --> 00:13:12.333
I talked to Richard
about it last night,

00:13:12.333 --> 00:13:13.334
best years of my life.
- Amen.

00:13:13.334 --> 00:13:14.376
- Like, we have our group chat.

00:13:14.376 --> 00:13:15.377
We talk every day.

00:13:15.377 --> 00:13:16.128
- Every day.

00:13:16.128 --> 00:13:17.254
- Every single day.

00:13:17.254 --> 00:13:20.966
- My memories of Cleveland
were so vivid,
- Yeah.
- right?

00:13:20.966 --> 00:13:22.384
And obviously, we won.

00:13:22.384 --> 00:13:26.222
But, like, I've never been so locked
in with a team to where it

00:13:26.222 --> 00:13:28.766
was like, we, like, loved each other.

00:13:28.766 --> 00:13:32.186
Mo Williams literally
left, and nobody

00:13:32.186 --> 00:13:34.104
knew where he went.
And then he came back

00:13:34.104 --> 00:13:36.273
and played in Game
5 of the NBA Finals.
- Yeah.

00:13:36.273 --> 00:13:38.067
- And was important to us.

00:13:38.067 --> 00:13:40.110
Dahntay Jones played
in the Finals.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:43.197
Like, I remember vividly Coach
going, "Dahntay, I need you."

00:13:43.197 --> 00:13:44.698
And he rips his stuff off.

00:13:44.698 --> 00:13:45.866
Hasn't played a minute.

00:13:45.866 --> 00:13:47.618
- Dahntay Jones was big for us.

00:13:47.618 --> 00:13:48.118
- Integral.

00:13:48.118 --> 00:13:48.619
- Game 6.

00:13:48.619 --> 00:13:50.204
- Integral, is that
the word, intregal?

00:13:50.204 --> 00:13:51.205
- Integral.

00:13:51.205 --> 00:13:52.373
- Integral.

00:13:52.373 --> 00:13:53.791
There we go. That's a good word.

00:13:53.791 --> 00:13:55.167
Mental health, right?

00:13:55.167 --> 00:13:56.877
It has taken off.

00:13:56.877 --> 00:14:00.631
And you have been the face.

00:14:00.631 --> 00:14:02.299
You are the front of
the train of that.

00:14:02.299 --> 00:14:07.096
So tell me what inspired
you to be the face of this

00:14:07.096 --> 00:14:09.431
and then why you
are the way you are.

00:14:09.431 --> 00:14:09.932
- Yeah.

00:14:09.932 --> 00:14:13.102
Well, first of all, it
goes back to, really,

00:14:13.102 --> 00:14:16.397
my childhood and
hiding it for, I mean,

00:14:16.397 --> 00:14:17.773
really till I was 29 years old.

00:14:17.773 --> 00:14:19.191
And that was 2017.
- Right.

00:14:19.191 --> 00:14:23.696
- And not exposing it, actually,
till 2018. March of that year.

00:14:23.696 --> 00:14:24.363
And it's funny.

00:14:24.363 --> 00:14:25.614
You're such a huge part of it.

00:14:25.614 --> 00:14:27.616
You know this, and a
lot of people don't, is

00:14:27.616 --> 00:14:31.120
that there were a lot of pillars
that had fallen in my life

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:36.208
personally, whether it be my
family, friends, relationship,

00:14:36.208 --> 00:14:37.459
business.

00:14:37.459 --> 00:14:38.752
I started self-medicating.

00:14:38.752 --> 00:14:42.715
I started getting
extremely dark.

00:14:42.715 --> 00:14:45.885
The game was taken away from
me from an injury perspective.

00:14:45.885 --> 00:14:50.306
I had broken my left hand
in a game in Detroit.

00:14:50.306 --> 00:14:51.390
- Oh, I remember that.

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:51.891
- Yeah.

00:14:51.891 --> 00:14:53.809
A lot of people didn't
know what was going on.

00:14:53.809 --> 00:14:58.480
So I had teammates and people
turn on me and call me out,

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:01.901
and I wasn't ready to expose
myself at that level yet.

00:15:01.901 --> 00:15:06.739
And then All-Star came around,
and Parkland, Florida, happened.

00:15:06.739 --> 00:15:09.033
And there were
rumblings at All-Star

00:15:09.033 --> 00:15:13.162
that were going on of certain
people trying to speak

00:15:13.162 --> 00:15:14.455
about me and my mental health.

00:15:14.455 --> 00:15:19.376
And I said, OK, now I
need to tell my story.

00:15:19.376 --> 00:15:21.170
I don't want anybody
else to tell my story.

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:23.756
I need to take this
into my own hands.

00:15:23.756 --> 00:15:24.548
I'm afraid.

00:15:24.548 --> 00:15:25.966
I'm afraid of how
people are going

00:15:25.966 --> 00:15:28.344
to react, how it's going to
affect my livelihood, what

00:15:28.344 --> 00:15:30.137
people are gonna think,
what my best friends are

00:15:30.137 --> 00:15:31.472
gonna think who don't
even know what the hell I’m

00:15:31.472 --> 00:15:32.348
going through.

00:15:32.348 --> 00:15:36.101
So that led me up to that point.

00:15:36.101 --> 00:15:38.270
I didn't pull back
the curtain that much,

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:41.106
but enough for people
to look inside and say,

00:15:41.106 --> 00:15:43.067
everybody is going
through something.

00:15:43.067 --> 00:15:45.319
And us athletes
are no different.

00:15:45.319 --> 00:15:47.363
Success is not
immune to depression.

00:15:47.363 --> 00:15:49.114
This thing doesn't discriminate.

00:15:49.114 --> 00:15:51.825
Look at the numbers, especially
coming out of this time too,

00:15:51.825 --> 00:15:53.202
the last 12 to 14 months.

00:15:53.202 --> 00:15:53.702
It’s just—

00:15:53.702 --> 00:15:56.622
- When you see death for real,
or when you deal with that,

00:15:56.622 --> 00:15:58.582
or when you're in
that place, you

00:15:58.582 --> 00:16:04.672
can't justify putting
your energy into a game.

00:16:04.672 --> 00:16:07.758
- Because honestly—
- I can't fight how I feel.

00:16:07.758 --> 00:16:11.762
- Different context, but
it was a similar feeling.

00:16:11.762 --> 00:16:15.140
That summer was very,
very, very dark for me.

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:15.641
- Yeah.

00:16:15.641 --> 00:16:17.226
- I had people literally
saying, like,

00:16:17.226 --> 00:16:19.770
“Don't take your life this summer.”

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:24.358
And that was crazy to me to
hear that, because I'm thinking,

00:16:24.358 --> 00:16:25.651
maybe I have blinders on.

00:16:25.651 --> 00:16:27.820
Maybe I'm not seeing what
these people are seeing.

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:29.738
So I really had to look
myself in the mirror,

00:16:29.738 --> 00:16:33.158
had to look inward that
summer and work on myself.

00:16:33.158 --> 00:16:37.162
But from then and earlier
that year to where I am now,

00:16:37.162 --> 00:16:39.873
I feel like being able to
just pay it forward, be

00:16:39.873 --> 00:16:41.250
able to speak about it

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:41.959
- For sure.

00:16:41.959 --> 00:16:44.086
- has been amazing.

00:16:44.086 --> 00:16:47.131
I think, honestly, sharing
your story not only empowers

00:16:47.131 --> 00:16:49.425
yourself, but is a way of
paying it forward for others.

00:16:49.425 --> 00:16:51.927
I always say nothing haunts us
like the things we don't say.

00:16:51.927 --> 00:16:53.762
You have to leave it out there.

00:16:53.762 --> 00:16:55.180
You have to expose
it in some way.

00:16:55.180 --> 00:16:56.932
I'm not saying you
have to do it publicly,

00:16:56.932 --> 00:17:00.185
but it's amazing how
freeing that can be.

00:17:00.185 --> 00:17:03.105
- The game is, "Do You
Know Your Best Friend?"
- You’re not my best friend.

00:17:03.105 --> 00:17:07.026
- Kevin, what is my
favorite city to play in?

00:17:07.026 --> 00:17:09.361
I'll be honest, I
don't even know what—

00:17:09.361 --> 00:17:09.987
- Phoenix?

00:17:09.987 --> 00:17:10.487
- Top three.

00:17:10.487 --> 00:17:10.988
OK.

00:17:10.988 --> 00:17:11.864
One.
[Ding]

00:17:11.864 --> 00:17:12.990
- Do you like Chicago?

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:13.532
- No.

00:17:13.532 --> 00:17:14.992
- Do you like New York City?

00:17:14.992 --> 00:17:16.285
- This is not how the game works.

00:17:16.285 --> 00:17:17.786
- OK. OK.

00:17:17.786 --> 00:17:18.829
- So Phoenix—

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:21.290
Phoenix, New York.
[Ding]

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:22.124
Can't say Cleveland.

00:17:22.124 --> 00:17:23.709
It's too obvious.

00:17:23.709 --> 00:17:24.418
Los Angeles?
[Ding]

00:17:24.418 --> 00:17:25.669
- Yeah.

00:17:25.669 --> 00:17:26.336
Boom.

00:17:26.336 --> 00:17:29.757
- Channing Frye, what
breed of dog do I have?

00:17:29.757 --> 00:17:31.842
- Oh, Vestry is a Vizsla.
[Ding]

00:17:31.842 --> 00:17:32.468
- There you go.

00:17:32.468 --> 00:17:33.594
- I hate that fucking dog.

00:17:33.594 --> 00:17:38.182
What is the one thing
that makes a good party?

00:17:38.182 --> 00:17:39.058
- A cheese platter.

00:17:39.058 --> 00:17:40.726
- A fucking cheese platter.

00:17:40.726 --> 00:17:41.643
- Got to hit it right.

00:17:41.643 --> 00:17:45.147
- If it is not local
sourced honey,

00:17:45.147 --> 00:17:46.648
I'm not fucking with you.

00:17:46.648 --> 00:17:48.400
- It's got to be the bee's knees.

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:51.487
Are we hearing that
in the background?

00:17:51.487 --> 00:17:53.864
What is my pregame ritual?

00:17:53.864 --> 00:17:54.364
- Oh.

00:17:54.364 --> 00:17:55.532
- This is impossible to—

00:17:55.532 --> 00:17:57.326
- You drink your beet juice.

00:17:57.326 --> 00:17:59.995
You take your little
peanut butter jelly thing.

00:17:59.995 --> 00:18:02.039
You get rid of
everything that's sweaty.

00:18:02.039 --> 00:18:05.417
You go through six pairs of
socks, two jerseys, two pants.

00:18:05.417 --> 00:18:07.628
You switch all your stuff
up because you're sweating.

00:18:07.628 --> 00:18:10.214
You used to do these
stretches and these stretches

00:18:10.214 --> 00:18:11.632
for at least 20 minutes.

00:18:11.632 --> 00:18:12.341
- ELDOA stretches.

00:18:12.341 --> 00:18:13.258
- ELDOs?

00:18:13.258 --> 00:18:14.384
- ELDOA.

00:18:14.384 --> 00:18:15.135
- ELDOA stretches?

00:18:15.135 --> 00:18:15.636
- Yeah.

00:18:15.636 --> 00:18:16.345
- So yes or no?

00:18:16.345 --> 00:18:17.471
Am I right or wrong?

00:18:17.471 --> 00:18:20.432
- Put some ‘speck on my name.

00:18:20.432 --> 00:18:22.351
- Kevin, how old are my kids?

00:18:22.351 --> 00:18:25.354
Oh, my God.

00:18:25.354 --> 00:18:26.855
Hendrix is 11.

00:18:26.855 --> 00:18:27.564
- 10.
[Buzzer]

00:18:27.564 --> 00:18:28.065
Close.

00:18:28.065 --> 00:18:30.150
- Margaux is seven.

00:18:30.150 --> 00:18:30.651
- Nine.
